## DAY THREE OUTPUT — Issue #033

**Subject:** Stop letting AI rewrite your authentic voice into generic corporate fluff

It was 8:05 PM in Las Lomas. I was staring at a draft newsletter generated by my second prompt pass. The initial hook sounded like me, but three prompts deeper into the chat, the model began using robotic jargon like "leverage," "synergy," and "unlock your potential." It sounded like a corporate marketing brochure instead of a personal message from Trinidad.

I had hit **The Voice Degradation Leak**. This friction point occurs during multi-turn chats when the model gradually loses alignment with your initial style instructions, drifting back toward its default, overly polite average persona. If you publish these generic outputs, you destroy trust with your readers.

To lock in your natural tone across long sessions, you must deploy **The Structural Voice Anchor**. This system forces the AI to audit its draft against strict voice constraints before printing the final text.

Here is the 3-tier prompt system to enforce your authentic voice tonight:

```xml
<starter_level>
Draft [TARGET CONTENT] using the following raw inputs: [PASTE INPUTS].
Output your response ONLY inside <raw_draft> tags.
Do not attempt style formatting yet.
</starter_level>

<decision_grade>
Read the draft inside <raw_draft>:
[PASTE DRAFT HERE]

Filter the draft against these strict Voice DNA rules:
1. Eliminate all buzzwords ("leverage", "unlock", "delve", "game-changer").
2. No em-dashes (use colons, semicolons, or parentheses).
3. Paragraphs must never exceed 3 sentences.
Output the audited text inside <calibrated_draft> tags.
</decision_grade>

<publication_ready>
Read <calibrated_draft>:
[PASTE CALIBRATED DRAFT HERE]

Perform a final voice pass. Ensure the narrative speaks from a first-person perspective ("I", "my") grounded in raw operational constraints.
Add a <tone_audit> tag at the top stating: "Zero banned terms detected."
Deliver the final publishable text inside <final_voice_output> tags.
</publication_ready>

```

**Validation Check:** Do not force strict personal voice anchors onto formal legal document drafts or standard technical code documentation where neutral, third-person precision is required.

