## DAY FOUR OUTPUT — Issue #034

**Subject:** How to stop tweaking prompts and actually ship your work tonight

It was 8:40 PM in Trinidad. My 3-hour execution window was closing fast. I was sitting at my desk, micro-editing the same prompt for the sixth time, trying to get the AI to output a mathematically perfect CTA sentence. Instead of shipping Issue #034 and closing down my room for the night, I was spinning my wheels tweaking minor word choices.

I fell directly into **The Endless Edit Loop**. This trap occurs when you use AI flexibility as an excuse for perfectionism, repeatedly requesting tiny revisions rather than accepting a 90% solution and shipping on schedule. You waste your precious surplus hours editing work that was already good enough to publish.

To crush this perfectionism, you must deploy **The Single-Pass Calibration Rule**. This framework caps your iterative cycles at two passes and forces an auto-correct decision based on a predefined grace rule.

Here is the 3-tier prompt system to lock in your final draft:

```xml
<starter_level>
Generate [TARGET ASSET] based on [CORE INPUTS].
Output the complete text inside <draft_version> tags.
Do not add conversational preamble.
</starter_level>

<decision_grade>
Review <draft_version>:
[PASTE DRAFT HERE]

Identify up to 3 minor errors (e.g., paragraph length, missing metric, word choice).
Fix them immediately in place. Do not rewrite structural components that already work.
Output the updated draft inside <fixed_version> tags.
</decision_grade>

<publication_ready>
Read <fixed_version>:
[PASTE FIXED VERSION HERE]

Perform a final readiness check. If minor style flaws remain, auto-correct them directly without requesting further feedback.
Add a <shipping_verdict> tag with value "READY TO PUBLISH".
Deliver the final asset inside <final_ship_container> tags.
</publication_ready>

```

**Validation Check:** Do not enforce hard single-pass limits when generating high-stakes legal contracts or financial agreements where an undiscovered error carries legal or financial liabilities.

